course: ai
lesson: 4
title: Stories, jokes and riddles with a chatbot
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Chatbots such as Gemini and ChatGPT are great fun for stories: ask for a dragon who is scared of the dark and a sweet bedtime story appears in seconds, because the bot has read thousands of fairy tales.
explain: Jokes and famous riddles come easily too, but they are often the oldest ones, like the scarecrow "outstanding in his field", because those appear most often in the text it learned from.
explain: Give it a brand-new riddle, a silly trick question or a big sum, and it may answer just as confidently and get it badly wrong.
explain: When a bot invents a confident answer that is not true, like a made-up head teacher or a book that does not exist, people call it a "hallucination".
explain: So play, laugh and enjoy beating the bot at your own riddles, and check anything that matters.
fact: Chatbots are good at writing stories because they learned from huge numbers of stories.
fact: Bots tend to repeat the most common jokes, because those appeared most often in their training text.
fact: Asking for a joke about something very specific, like your own hamster, pushes a bot away from the oldest jokes.
fact: Knowing the answer to a famous riddle is not the same as solving a new one.
fact: Bots see words in chunks rather than single letters, so counting letters, like the r's in "strawberry", can trip them up.
fact: "Strawberry" has three r's.
fact: Some chatbots guess numbers like words, so big sums can come out wrong; check with a calculator.
fact: A confident made-up answer from a bot is called a hallucination.
fact: Bots can invent book titles and authors that sound real but do not exist.
fact: Long extra explanations can hide mistakes, even when the main answer is right.
fact: Bots have no feelings to hurt, but polite asking keeps your own good habits strong.
fact: How you talk to machines can become how you talk to people.
exercise: Mia asks Gemini for a dragon bedtime story and gets a lovely one fast. Why is the bot good at this? | It has learned from a huge number of stories | It has met real dragons on its travels around the world | A writer keeps stories ready for children who ask | It copies out the one story book it was given
exercise: Jake gets the scarecrow joke he has heard a hundred times. Why that joke? | Common jokes appear most often in what bots learned | The bot thinks it is the funniest joke anyone has ever told | Bots are only allowed to tell one joke | The scarecrow joke was written by the bot
exercise: The bot knows the piano riddle but fails on Sam's new riddle. Why? | It knew the famous answer, but new riddles need real reasoning | Sam's riddle was against the rules that all bots have to follow | The bot was tired after the first riddle | New riddles are impossible for anyone
exercise: The bot says "strawberry" has two r's. How many does it have? | Three | Two | One | Four
exercise: Why can letter-counting trip up a chatbot? | It sees words in chunks, not letter by letter | It cannot read the letter r at all, because r is a tricky letter | It is too proud to count | Letters are too small for computers to see
exercise: Connor asks the bot for 37 times 48 and it confidently gives a wrong number. What should he do? | Check it with a calculator or by hand | Trust it, because bots never get sums wrong | Ask the same bot again and again until it agrees | Use the wrong number, it's close enough
exercise: The bot gives Chloe a confident but made-up name for her head teacher. What is this called? | A hallucination | A memory | A secret password | A translation
exercise: Two of the three horse books the bot suggested don't exist. What should Maddie do? | Look the titles up in a library catalogue | Ask the library to buy the two missing books | Write the two books herself so that they exist | Believe the bot, the library must be wrong
exercise: Sam asks "What colour was Napoleon's white horse?" and gets "white" plus a long history lesson. What is the risk? | The long extra part may hide mistakes | The answer "white" must be a hallucination | Napoleon never actually had a horse at all | History lessons from bots are always perfect
exercise: How many legs does a spider have, shoes or no shoes? | Eight | Sixteen | Six | Ten
exercise: Mia says please and thank you to the bot; her brother says it's pointless. What is fair? | The bot isn't hurt either way, but politeness keeps her habits kind | Her brother is right, manners are only for people and never for machines | The bot works only if you say please | The bot will refuse to help her brother
exercise: Connor shouts insults at the voice assistant and it stays calm. What is the point to notice? | It isn't hurt, but the shouting habit is Connor's own | The assistant secretly records it all to tell his mum later | The assistant felt very hurt but hid it | Shouting makes the assistant work faster
exercise: Why do strange, silly questions often get silly answers? | They break the patterns the bot learned | Bots always refuse to be serious with children | Silly questions use up all of its battery power | The bot is just joking on purpose every time
exercise: How can you get a fresher joke from a bot? | Ask for one about something very specific, like your hamster | Ask the same question ten times in a row and take the last one | Shout the question so it tries harder | Ask for "the most famous joke ever"
test: "this bot's a genius, it got the piano riddle" What would you test it with? | A brand-new riddle you made up yourself | The same piano riddle again, to be sure | A riddle from a famous book | A question about pianos
test: Your mate says, "bot said strawberry's got 2 r's so that's that". Put him right. | Count it yourself: s-t-r-a-w-b-e-r-r-y has three | He's right, bots count letters perfectly every time | It has one r, both are wrong | It depends on how you say it
test: What would happen if Maddie took the bot's book list straight to the library without checking? | She might hunt for books that don't exist | The library would print the books for her on the spot | The librarian would know the bot was lying | The books would appear on the shelf
test: A cartoon shows a bot doing 37 × 48 in its head and always being right. What should you think? | Real chatbots can get big sums wrong, so check | That is true, bots are perfect calculators | Bots can't do sums at all, not even 1 + 1 | Only cartoons can do sums
test: The bot tells Chloe her head teacher is "Mr Whitfield". There is no Mr Whitfield. Why did this happen? | It didn't know, so it made up a likely-sounding name | Mr Whitfield secretly works at the school and nobody knows | The bot read the school's private files | The head teacher changed his name last week
test: "stop saying please to it, it's a machine, you muppet" What is the best reply? | It's not for the machine, it keeps my manners in practice | It will stop working properly if I'm rude to it even once | Machines cry when you are rude | Saying please makes the answers longer and much better too
test: Jake asks about a spider "counting the shoes" and the bot says 16. What does that show? | Silly trick questions can confuse the bot | Spiders really do have 16 legs when they wear shoes | Spiders always wear eight shoes | The bot is right and Jake is wrong
test: Why do bots tell the same old jokes again and again? | Those jokes appear most in what they learned | Bots have only one joke book, and they read it again and again | Old jokes are the only funny ones | Bots get paid more for old jokes
test: Your little cousin shouts rude words at the speaker for fun. What could you gently say? | Habits stick; talking rudely can spread to how you talk to people | The speaker will feel really sad and cry, and then after a while it will break | The speaker will call the police | It is fine, nothing we do ever becomes a habit
test: Which use of a chatbot is safest without checking? | Making up a silly bedtime story for fun | Getting the answer to a big sum for homework | Finding out the name of your new head teacher | Listing real books to read for a school project
test: "bots never make stuff up, they just look it up" Is that right? | No, they can make up confident answers that are false | Yes, they look everything up in a big encyclopedia first | Yes, every answer is copied from a website | No, they never answer questions at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "hallucination" as applied to a chatbot? | The confident production of an answer that is untrue | The perception of images by the chatbot during sleep | The deliberate concealment of a correct answer | The repetition of a joke previously heard by the user
exam: It is given that a chatbot reports that the word "strawberry" contains two instances of the letter r. Ascertain the correct number of such instances. | Three | Two | One | Four
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a chatbot reproduces well-known jokes in preference to novel ones. | Such jokes occur most frequently in the text from which it learned | It is required by its makers to repeat a single joke | It regards novel jokes as unsuitable for children | It is incapable of producing any sentence not seen before
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the proper treatment of a list of books furnished by a chatbot. | The existence of each title ought to be verified before reliance is placed upon it | Each title may be presumed to exist without verification | The list ought to be disregarded in its entirety without examination | The titles exist only if the chatbot repeats them twice
